  ![AI](https://img.etimg.com/thumb/msid-130209589,width-650,height-488,imgsize-40796,resizemode-75/ai.jpg)iStockA CEO got his comeuppance after replacing his team with AI
  
  At a time when artificial intelligence is increasingly being seen as a threat to engineering roles, many industry voices continue to stress that machines cannot fully substitute human judgment, creativity, and oversight. A recent incident shared by a software professional on the social media platform X highlighted how an overreliance on automation led to severe financial consequences for a company.
  
  ### Replacing Humans with Automation
  
  According to the developer’s account, the company’s leadership decided to eliminate its entire quality assurance division, which consisted of twelve members. The move was aimed at cutting costs, with management introducing an AI-driven automated testing system expected to save approximately $1.2 million annually. However, this decision quickly proved to be flawed.
  
  ### A Costly Technical Failure
  
  Soon after the transition, the automated system reportedly generated an erroneous discount code due to a malfunction often described as “hallucination” in AI systems. This glitch resulted in products across the company’s platform being priced at zero, leading to a massive loss of nearly $6 million in orders. The situation underscored the risks of deploying AI without adequate human supervision, especially in critical operational areas like testing and validation.
  
  > — shazcodes (@shazcodes)
  
  ### Irony and Backlash
  
  What made the situation even more controversial was the leadership’s response. Instead of addressing the issue internally, the CEO allegedly asked a senior developer to contact the former head of the QA team—someone who had recently been laid off—and request assistance in resolving the crisis without compensation. This move sparked criticism online, with many pointing out the ethical and professional implications of expecting unpaid help from displaced employees.
  
  ### Public Reaction and Industry Concerns
  
  The post quickly gained traction, amassing over a million views and tens of thousands of likes. Users across the platform expressed frustration over what they perceived as shortsighted corporate behavior. Many argued that companies rushing to replace human workers entirely with AI are not truly reducing costs but merely postponing inevitable consequences. Others noted that such trends are becoming increasingly common, with QA professionals often being pushed toward development roles in anticipation of automation taking over their existing responsibilities.
  
  ### A Broader Lesson
  
  The incident serves as a cautionary tale about the limitations of artificial intelligence. While AI can enhance efficiency, it cannot yet replicate the nuanced understanding and accountability that human professionals bring to complex systems.
  
