  An Oxford Economics report from January found that many layoffs CEOs called AI-related were actually the result of past overhiring. A Carnegie ...

  --- Content ---
  [Money](https://www.forbes.com/money/)[Investing](https://www.forbes.com/investing/)
  
  # Why Today’s AI-Driven Layoffs Are Becoming Tomorrow’s Rehiring Crisis
  
  By [Jon Markman](https://www.forbes.com/sites/fast-forward-investing/ "https://www.forbes.com/sites/fast-forward-investing/"),
  
  Contributor.
  
  Forbes contributors publish independent expert analyses and insights.
  Analyzing tech stocks through the prism of cultural change.
  
  Follow Author
  
  Mar 04, 2026, 09:26am EST
  
  0
  
  ![GERMANY-LAYOFFS-TECH-CHINA-UNIONS-LABOUR](https://imageio.forbes.com/specials-images/imageserve/69a8409452c98a9c0d879104/GERMANY-LAYOFFS-TECH-CHINA-UNIONS-LABOUR/0x0.jpg?width=960)
  
  Employees of social media and online video platform TikTok display a banner reading 'We trained your machines - pay us what we deserve' during a protest called by the Ver.di union outside TikTok's offices in Berlin on July 17, 2025, to demonstrate against a threat of layoffs that specifically concerns content moderators of TikTok Germany and the closure of the Trust and Safety Department. (Photo by John MACDOUGALL / AFP) (Photo by JOHN MACDOUGALL/AFP via Getty Images)... More
  
  AFP via Getty Images
  
  ## Amazon, Nike, and Home Depot Cut 17,575 Jobs in One Week. Gartner Says Half the Companies Making Cuts Like These Will Be Rehiring by 2027.
  
  The last week of January 2026 earned the nickname ["Automation Week"](https://www.growthfactor.ai/newsletter/the-automation-week-33-000-jobs-cut-as-retailers-restructure-for-ai) in retail circles. Amazon [slashed 16,000 corporate roles](https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/28/tech/amazon-layoffs-ai), its second mass layoff since October. Nike [cut 775 distribution center jobs](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/26/nike-to-lay-off-775-employees-at-us-distribution-centers.html) in Tennessee and Mississippi. Home Depot [eliminated 800 positions](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/28/home-depot-layoffs-return-to-office.html), mostly in its technology organization. Three companies. One week. 17,575 jobs gone.
  
  Then, on February 3, Gartner published a prediction that should make every board of directors pause: [by 2027, half of companies that attributed headcount reductions to AI will rehire staff](https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-02-03-gartner-predicts-half-of-companies-that-cut-customer-service-staff-due-to-ai-will-rehire-by-2027) to perform similar functions, often under different job titles. Gartner's October 2025 survey of 321 customer service leaders found that [only 20% had actually reduced staffing because of AI](https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-02-03-gartner-predicts-half-of-companies-that-cut-customer-service-staff-due-to-ai-will-rehire-by-2027). Most cuts were driven by broader economic conditions. Forrester's data is even sharper: [55% of employers already regret laying off workers for AI](https://www.metaintro.com/blog/ai-job-cuts-reverse-2027).
  
  The question is which of these companies are making a calculated transformation and which are buying a stock pop they'll pay for later.
  
  PROMOTED
  
  ### The Amazon Case Is More Complicated Than It Looks
  
  The original draft of this article described Amazon as "the gold standard" executing a plan built on "solid bedrock." The reality is messier.
  
  Amazon did cut 16,000 corporate roles in late January, on top of [14,000 in October](https://www.careerflow.ai/blog/amazon-layoffs-2026), bringing total reductions to [over 30,000 since CEO Andy Jassy first signaled AI-driven organizational changes](https://abcnews.com/Business/wireStory/artificial-intelligence-drive-layoffs-amazon-firms-hard-129773731). The company is also [closing all 14 Amazon Go stores and 50+ Amazon Fresh locations](https://www.careerflow.ai/blog/amazon-layoffs-2026) to consolidate around Whole Foods. It spent [$125 billion on data centers in 2025](https://www.careerflow.ai/blog/amazon-layoffs-2026) and plans $200 billion in capital expenditures, mostly targeting AWS AI workloads.
  
  MORE FOR YOU
  
  ### [Mets’ Bo Bichette Offers 4-Word Response On Returning To Blue Jays Role](http://www.forbes.com/sites/peterchawaga/2026/05/03/mets-bo-bichette-offers-4-word-response-on-returning-to-blue-jays-role/ "Mets’ Bo Bichette Offers 4-Word Response On Returning To Blue Jays Role")
  
  ### [Madonna Reaches One Billboard Chart For The First Time In Her Career](http://www.forbes.com/sites/hughmcintyre/2026/05/03/madonna-reaches-one-billboard-chart-for-the-first-time-in-her-career/ "Madonna Reaches One Billboard Chart For The First Time In Her Career")
  
  ### [Motorola Beats Apple And Samsung: The Razr Ultra 2026 Debuts Innovative Battery Tech](http://www.forbes.com/sites/davidphelan/2026/05/03/motorola-beats-apple-and-samsung-the-razr-ultra-2026-debuts-game-changing-battery-tech/ "Motorola Beats Apple And Samsung: The Razr Ultra 2026 Debuts Innovative Battery Tech")
  
  But Amazon itself [told CNN that AI isn't the reason behind the "vast majority" of the cuts](https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/31/tech/amazon-layoffs-january-tech-changes). SVP Beth Galetti framed them as ["reducing layers, increasing ownership, and removing bureaucracy"](https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/28/tech/amazon-layoffs-ai). A Rutgers professor quoted by CNN made a point that undercuts the "solid bedrock" narrative: ["Amazon cannot easily retrain a workforce built for manual logistics or legacy retail systems into one that builds generative AI agents."](https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/31/tech/amazon-layoffs-january-tech-changes)
  
  One laid-off Amazon employee [put it more bluntly to ABC News](https://abcnews.com/Business/wireStory/artificial-intelligence-drive-layoffs-amazon-firms-hard-129773731): "When you reduce head count, you've demonstrated efficiency, you attract more capital, the share price goes up. So you could potentially have just been bloated in the first place, reduce head count, attribute it to AI, and now you've got a value story."
  
