  Amazon does have better data infrastructure than most retailers. It has decades of structured e-commerce data, AWS as a competitive advantage, and genuine AI deployment across fulfillment and logistics. But characterizing 30,000 layoffs as a surgical AI-driven transformation overstates what even Amazon is willing to claim about itself. A significant portion is post-pandemic right-sizing that would have happened regardless of AI.
  
  ### Nike and Home Depot: Not Really AI Stories
  
  Nike's 775 distribution center cuts deserve scrutiny because the automation narrative obscures what's actually happening. CNBC [reported](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/26/nike-to-lay-off-775-employees-at-us-distribution-centers.html) that the distribution capacity Nike built during its direct-to-consumer push under former CEO John Donahoe now exceeds current volume after the company pivoted back to wholesale partners under CEO Elliott Hill. Morningstar analyst David Swartz [told Reuters](https://news.designrush.com/nike-job-cuts-automation-distribution-centers-brand-strategy) that Nike likely "overbuilt warehouse capacity" given its recent sales declines. The company [reported a 32% drop in net income](https://news.designrush.com/nike-job-cuts-automation-distribution-centers-brand-strategy) in its most recent quarter.
  
  Nike's cuts are 775 distribution workers at a company with 77,800 employees. That's 1% of headcount, driven primarily by overcapacity from a failed strategic pivot, not by robots replacing humans. The company used the word "automation" in its statement, but [CNBC noted](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/01/26/nike-to-lay-off-775-employees-at-us-distribution-centers.html) it remains unclear exactly how Nike plans to expand automation and how much of a role it played in the cuts.
  
  Home Depot's 800 cuts are even less of an AI story. The company [confirmed to Retail Dive](https://www.retaildive.com/news/home-depot-cuts-eight-hundred-jobs/810796/) that the reductions were concentrated in its technology organization, paired with a five-day return-to-office mandate. CEO Ted Decker has been transparent about the real driver: housing activity is [at 40-year lows](https://www.thestreet.com/retail/home-depot-job-cuts) as a percentage of housing stock, and the demand inflection the company expected [never materialized](https://www.thestreet.com/retail/home-depot-job-cuts). Home Depot's fiscal 2026 guidance projects [flat to 2% comparable sales growth](https://www.metaintro.com/blog/home-depot-layoffs-return-office-2026). These are cyclical cuts at a company waiting for housing to recover, not an AI transformation.
  
  ### The Klarna Boomerang Is Real, and It Matters
  
  The original article got the Klarna story roughly right, but the details are more instructive than the summary suggested.
  
  Between 2022 and 2024, Klarna eliminated [approximately 700 customer service positions](https://www.entrepreneur.com/business-news/klarna-ceo-reverses-course-by-hiring-more-humans-not-ai/491396) and replaced them with an AI assistant built with OpenAI. CEO Sebastian Siemiatkowski [boasted that AI could do the work of 700 agents](https://www.reworked.co/employee-experience/klarna-claimed-ai-was-doing-the-work-of-700-people-now-its-rehiring/) and had taken on 75% of customer interactions. The company imposed a hiring freeze for over a year. Headcount dropped 22% to 3,500, mostly through attrition.
  
  By early 2025, the experiment had visibly failed. Customer complaints rose. Satisfaction ratings dropped. Users reported [robotic responses and the frustrating loop of repeating their issue to a human after the bot failed](https://www.reworked.co/employee-experience/klarna-claimed-ai-was-doing-the-work-of-700-people-now-its-rehiring/). In May 2025, Siemiatkowski [told Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2025-05-08/klarna-turns-from-ai-to-real-person-customer-service) that the company had gone too far: "We focused too much on efficiency and cost. The result was lower quality, and that's not sustainable."
  
  Klarna reversed course and began rehiring, targeting students and rural workers for remote, flexible customer service roles. The company now frames human support as [a competitive advantage, not a cost center](https://www.fintechweekly.com/magazine/articles/klarna-hires-customer-service-after-ai-pivot). This pivot happened just before Klarna's U.S. IPO, where shares surged 30% and the company hit a [$19.65 billion valuation](https://mlq.ai/news/klarna-ceo-admits-aggressive-ai-job-cuts-went-too-far-starts-hiring-again-after-us-ipo/).
  
  ### Block Just Made the Same Bet at 10x the Scale
  
  Five days ago, Block CEO Jack Dorsey cut [4,000 employees, roughly 40% of the company's workforce](https://www.cnn.com/2026/02/26/business/block-layoffs-ai-jack-dorsey), and was explicit about the reason: AI. Block's stock [surged up to 24%](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/02/26/block-laying-off-about-4000-employees-nearly-half-of-its-workforce.html). Dorsey told analysts that ["something happened in December of last year where the models just got an order of magnitude more capable"](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/jack-dorsey-block-slashes-nearly-041816081.html) and predicted that ["within the next year, the majority of companies will reach the same conclusion."](https://fortune.com/2026/02/27/block-jack-dorsey-ceo-xyz-stock-square-4000-ai-layoffs/)
  
  The market loved it. But consider the context. Block's stock had [dropped roughly 40% since early 2025](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/jack-dorsey-block-slashes-nearly-041816081.html). The company had [nearly tripled its workforce from 2019 to 2023](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/jack-dorsey-block-slashes-nearly-041816081.html), continued hiring a full year longer than peers like Shopify and Coinbase, and had gone through multiple rounds of performance-based cuts already. Dorsey [admitted on X](https://sfstandard.com/2026/02/26/block-lays-off-staff/) that he "over-hired during covid because I incorrectly built 2 separate company structures (Square & Cash App)." The company also [spent $68 million](https://fortune.com/2026/03/04/block-layoffs-jack-dorsey-ai-jobs-outlook-for-ceos/) five months ago flying everyone in for an in-person company event with Jay-Z.
  
  Block offered little detail on exactly how its AI tools are making specific roles unnecessary. Bloomberg [noted](https://finance.yahoo.com/news/jack-dorsey-block-slashes-nearly-041816081.html) that some analysts have questioned whether the company is "genuinely being transformed by AI or simply using it as a convenient rationale for cost cuts they would have made anyway."
  
  This is the pattern Gartner is flagging: companies announce AI-driven layoffs, the stock pops, and the actual AI capabilities lag behind the narrative. The layoffs are real. The AI transformation that supposedly justified them is still a work in progress.
  
  ### What's Actually Happening Versus the Story Being Told
  
  The data from the first quarter of 2026 tells a clearer story than any individual company announcement. U.S. employers announced [over 61,650 job cuts in the last week of January alone](https://www.fastcompany.com/91483264/layoffs-update-list-2026-amazon-nike-dow-ups-brutal-january-job-cuts). Challenger, Gray & Christmas reports that [January layoff announcements hit the highest level since 2009](https://www.newsnationnow.com/business/major-employers-job-cuts-2026/). AI was responsible for [54,836 announced layoff plans in 2025](https://www.hcamag.com/us/specialization/recruitment/when-ai-redundancies-backfire-employers-now-scrambling-to-rehire-humans/564000) and has been cited in 71,825 job cut announcements since 2023.
  
  But Gartner's survey reveals the gap between narrative and reality. Only 20% of customer service leaders who reduced headcount did so because of AI. The rest were responding to economic pressures and using AI as convenient framing. As Gartner analyst Kathy Ross [put it](https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-02-03-gartner-predicts-half-of-companies-that-cut-customer-service-staff-due-to-ai-will-rehire-by-2027): "Most recent workforce reductions were influenced by broader economic conditions rather than automation alone."
  
  A Vanguard report [found](https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/28/tech/amazon-layoffs-ai) that jobs highly exposed to AI automation are actually growing faster than they did before the pandemic, and faster than all other occupations. An Oxford Economics report from January [found](https://fortune.com/2026/02/27/block-jack-dorsey-ceo-xyz-stock-square-4000-ai-layoffs/) that many layoffs CEOs called AI-related were actually the result of past overhiring. A Carnegie Mellon study found that [even the best AI workers could only complete about a quarter of basic tasks](https://www.metaintro.com/blog/ai-job-cuts-reverse-2027) assigned to them.
  
  The boomerang is already visible. Klarna is rehiring. Gartner predicts half of AI-cutting companies will follow by 2027. The 5.3% rehire rate of terminated employees, [tracked by analytics firm Visier](https://www.hcamag.com/us/specialization/recruitment/when-ai-redundancies-backfire-employers-now-scrambling-to-rehire-humans/564000), is already ticking up. Companies that mistake a stock pop for a strategy will pay for it in customer churn, operational gaps, and the recruiting costs of bringing back the institutional knowledge they just showed out the door.
  
  ### How to Read This
  
  Separate the companies making structural transformations from the ones making announcements. Amazon has genuine AI infrastructure and a plausible path forward, even if its layoffs are also about post-pandemic bloat. Nike and Home Depot are dealing with cyclical business problems that have almost nothing to do with AI. Block is making a bet that even its own CEO frames as unprecedented and risky.
  
  The stocks that pop on layoff announcements are pricing in margin expansion. Whether that expansion materializes depends on whether the AI replacing those workers actually works. For every Meta building a $10 billion AI ad engine, there's a Klarna quietly calling back the humans it fired. The boomerang is not a prediction. It's already happening.
  
