  # You Were Not Replaced by AI. You Were Replaced by a CEO Who Wanted a Headline.
  
  ## A Fintech giant fired 700 people, bragged about it on every stage, then quietly asked them back at lower wages. The AI revolution has an accountability problem nobody wants to name.
  
  [![MayhemCode](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fill:32:32/1*yBtBwpzHDooPNdVbY24HGA.jpeg)](https://medium.com/@mayhemcode?source=post_page---byline--12f305e695aa---------------------------------------)
  
  [MayhemCode](https://medium.com/@mayhemcode?source=post_page---byline--12f305e695aa---------------------------------------)
  
  Follow
  
  12 min read
  
  ·
  
  Feb 25, 2026
  
  471
  
  5
  
  [Listen](https://medium.com/m/signin?actionUrl=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2Fplans%3Fdimension%3Dpost_audio_button%26postId%3D12f305e695aa&operation=register&redirect=https%3A%2F%2Fmedium.com%2Fcodex%2Fyou-were-not-replaced-by-ai-you-were-replaced-by-a-ceo-who-wanted-a-headline-12f305e695aa&source=---header_actions--12f305e695aa---------------------post_audio_button------------------)
  
  Share
  
  Let me start with a number. **Five hundred and seventy eight days.**
  
  That is roughly how long it took for Klarna’s CEO Sebastian Siemiatkowski to go from publicly declaring, with the confidence of someone who had never once worried about a rent payment, that **“AI can already do all of the jobs that we as humans do”**… to sheepishly telling Bloomberg that his company had focused “too much on cost” and was now recruiting humans again because the whole thing had produced, in his own words, **“** [**lower quality.**](https://www.mayhemcode.com/2026/02/ai-layoffs-2025-2026-why-companies-that.html) **”**
  
  Five hundred and seventy eight days. And in those five hundred and seventy eight days, [**700 people lost their jobs**](https://www.mayhemcode.com/2026/02/ai-layoffs-2025-2026-why-companies-that.html) **.** Over a thousand more were quietly managed out through a hiring freeze and what the company politely called “natural attrition.” Families were disrupted. Careers were derailed. People moved cities, moved back in with parents, made decisions that changed the shape of their lives, all because a CEO wanted a headline about being an AI-first company and a board that rewarded him for delivering one.
  
  Access without medium partner : [AI Layoffs — Rehiring at lower wages](https://www.mayhemcode.com/2026/02/ai-layoffs-2025-2026-why-companies-that.html)
  
  Press enter or click to view image in full size
  
  ![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*lEG--tNscXvxdq6b_2-Cbw.png)
  
  And now he wants them back. Not at their old salaries. Not with their old benefits. Klarna’s brilliant new plan is an **“Uber-style” workforce model.** Gig workers. Flexible hours. No stability. The empathy they amputated, now available for purchase at contractor rates.
  
  I am supposed to be balanced about this. I am going to try. But let me be honest with you first: **I find the audacity genuinely difficult to process.**
  
  ## The Headline Nobody Wanted to Write
  
  For about eighteen months, Klarna was the darling example of the AI productivity story. Every tech newsletter, every investor memo, every LinkedIn thought leader who had recently discovered the word “disruption” cited Klarna as proof that the future was here. The AI chatbot handled **2.3**…
  
